    "raw_cut_kept_for_reference": "`additive_raw` / `middle_raw` / `nonadditive_raw` in `panels`",
    "reporting_rule": "User decision 2026-09-21: the SCORED panels are `additive` and `nonadditive` (effect-controlled). `all` is still computed because cell-eval scores every cohort pair once per arm and the aggregation is free, but it is not the headline.",
    "statistic": "residual = ||delta_sum_of_singles - delta_real|| / ||delta_real||, whole-cohort pseudobulk on the 8,000-gene axis, controls = all 9,702 panel controls",
    "thresholds": {
      "q_hi": 0.5574290553728739,
      "q_lo": 0.4217733045419057
    },
    "why_effect_controlled": "The plain residual is a RELATIVE error, so it inflates when ||delta_real|| is small: spearman(residual, ||delta_real||) = -0.514, and the raw terciles put weak-effect pairs in `nonadditive` (median ||delta|| 2.39 vs 3.91). Effect size drives all four metrics, so a model comparison across the raw strata would be partly weak-vs-strong-effect rather than additive-vs-non-additive \u2014 meanpert's MAE is LOWER on the raw nonadditive panel (0.01245 vs 0.01484) purely because there is less signal there. Taking residual terciles WITHIN effect-size terciles drops spearman(stratum, ||delta_real||) to -0.090 and keeps the additivity separation (residual median 0.373 vs 0.639).",
    "why_not_pearson_r_alone": "r misses the MAGNITUDE failure mode: spearman(r, |mag-1|) = -0.255 while spearman(residual, |mag-1|) = +0.740. CEBPE+ZC3HAV1 has r 0.758 but the additive prediction is 2.42x too large."
  },
